From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:35:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] ios: ANGLE does not get native PVRTC, and say the extensions

0035 put PVRTC through to the host untouched. On ANGLE that produces a
black game: Wipeout, Wipeout 2 and Bejeweled drew their entire artwork
black while their text and uncompressed textures were perfect.

The failure has no symptom to catch. ANGLE advertises
GL_IMG_texture_compression_pvrtc, takes every glCompressedTexImage2D
without raising an error, and then samples black -- the run's log has
not one REFUSED or GL error from 145 native uploads, all square and
power of two. Its ES 1.1 is emulated over its own core and compressed
formats evidently do not survive the trip.

With nothing to detect, the renderer has to be named. The iPhone's own
OpenGL ES (gl-renderer=es3) is the driver these formats are native to,
so it is still free to try; ANGLE decodes as it always did and says why.

Also prints GL_EXTENSIONS on every renderer, not just the non-ANGLE EAGL
build. Working out what a host actually offers should not need a
custom build.

---
  hw/arm/gles-host.c | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++++---
  1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/arm/gles-host.c b/hw/arm/gles-host.c
index 7a5f965..3aec68e 100644
--- a/hw/arm/gles-host.c
+++ b/hw/arm/gles-host.c
@@ -2934,11 +2934,31 @@ static bool gles_pvrtc_native(void)
             return false;
         }
         /* A context is current by the time any upload runs, so the string is here to be read. */
+        const char *renderer = (const char *)glGetString(GL_RENDERER);
         ext = (const char *)glGetString(GL_EXTENSIONS);
         on = ext && strstr(ext, "texture_compression_pvrtc") ? 1 : 0;
-        fprintf(stderr, "[gles] PVRTC textures stay compressed: %s\n",
-                on ? "yes (the host GL takes them)"
-                   : "NO -- this host GL does not advertise PVRTC, so they are decoded as before");
+        /*
+         * ANGLE is the exception, and it has to be named rather than detected: it advertises PVRTC, accepts every
+         * upload without raising a single error, and then samples black. Update 87's run had Wipeout, Wipeout 2
+         * and Bejeweled draw their whole artwork black while their text and uncompressed textures were perfect,
+         * with no GL error anywhere in the log -- there is nothing to catch at upload time, so the only honest
+         * check is which renderer this is. Its ES 1.1 is emulated over its own core and compressed formats do not
+         * survive that trip.
+         *
+         * The iPhone's own OpenGL ES (gl-renderer=es3) is the driver these formats are native to, so it is left
+         * free to try.
+         */
+        if (on && renderer && strstr(renderer, "ANGLE")) {
+            on = 0;
+            fprintf(stderr, "[gles] PVRTC textures stay compressed: NO -- this renderer says it takes them and "
+                    "then draws them black, so they are decoded as before. The iPhone's own OpenGL ES "
+                    "(Settings > Graphics) is the one to try this on.\n");
+        } else {
+            fprintf(stderr, "[gles] PVRTC textures stay compressed: %s\n",
+                    on ? "yes (the host GL takes them)"
+                       : "NO -- this host GL does not advertise PVRTC, so they are decoded as before");
+        }
+        if (ext) fprintf(stderr, "[gles] host extensions: %s\n", ext);
     }
     return on == 1;
 }
